<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-edge-cases-decisions">
  <title>Edge Cases &amp; Human Decisions</title>
  
  <objectives>
    <ul>
      <li>Separate screening, segmentation, interpretation, matching, and correctness decisions.</li>
      <li>Handle common non-standard responses without repairing or discarding evidence.</li>
      <li>Distinguish ambiguity, out-of-vocabulary language, and codebook no-match cases.</li>
      <li>Classify disagreements at the earliest point where two analyses diverge.</li>
      <li>Write evidence-based notes and preserve genuinely unresolved cases.</li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <introduction>

    <p>
      An <term>edge case</term> is a response for which the ordinary workflow does
      not yield an immediate, high-confidence decision. The difficulty may concern
      screening, segmentation, component interpretation, matching, correctness,
      goal coverage, or the adequacy of the governing materials.
    </p>

    <p>
      Edge cases are not noise. They may expose ambiguity in a response, overlap in
      the codebook, missing vocabulary, an unstated mathematical assumption, or a
      gap in the protocol. Preserve the evidence, identify the exact difficult
      decision, and resolve, escalate, or retain the case as unresolved according
      to the current rules.
    </p>

    <p>
      Apply the evidence-first and no-repair principles from
      <xref ref="ch-coding-workflow" text="title"/>. This chapter gives the
      consequences of those principles for unusual cases rather than redefining
      the Episode Model.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-decision-types">
    <title>Major Decision Types</title>
    <p>
      Difficult cases become easier to discuss when the decision types remain separate.
      A response can be easy to screen but difficult to segment, easy to extract but
      difficult to match, or mathematically correct while providing no codable
      justification. Begin by naming the decision at issue rather than describing the
      entire response as <q>confusing</q> or <q>wrong.</q>
    </p>
    <table xml:id="tab-edge-major-decisions">
      <title>Major human decision points.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="20%"/>
        <col width="42%"/>
        <col width="38%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell><p>Decision</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Main question</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Possible outcome</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Screening</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Does the response contain analyzable justification?</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Include, exclude, or route for review.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Segmentation</p></cell>
          <cell><p>How many episodes are present, and where are their boundaries?</p></cell>
          <cell><p>One, several, incomplete, or unresolved.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Interpretation</p></cell>
          <cell><p>What are the criterion, inferential link, and judgment?</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Record a supported reading, an alternative reading, or a missing component.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Matching</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Which working-codebook entries fit the episode?</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Primary match, alternate match, no match, or vocabulary review.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Correctness</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Is the criterion true, and is the inference valid for this question?</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Assign the two digits when eligible, or leave them blank.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Goal coverage</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Which requested outputs does the response address?</p></cell>
          <cell><p>The goal identifier(s) addressed, <c>off-target</c>, or <c>unclear</c> with a note.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell><p>Framework revision</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Does the case expose a problem in the vocabulary, codebook, examples, or protocol?</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Propose clarification, gather similar cases, or preserve as unresolved.</p></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-decision-screening">
      <title>Screening Decisions</title>
      <p>
Apply Step 1 of <xref ref="ch-coding-workflow" text="title"/>. Screening
concerns codability, not correctness: an incorrect explanation may be
analyzable, while a correct bare answer may contain no codable justification.
</p>
<p>
Preserve blank, bare-answer, restatement, operation-only, hedged, and
conditional responses according to the playbook below. Describe what is
visible; do not infer the justification the student probably intended.
</p>
</paragraphs>
    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-decision-segmentation">
      <title>Segmentation Decisions</title>
      <p>
        Segmentation asks how many criterion-to-judgment moves are present. Connector
        words are useful signals, but sentence boundaries and connector counts do not
        determine episode boundaries. One sentence can contain several chained moves,
        and several sentences can elaborate one move.
      </p>
      <p>
        When two segmentations are plausible, record the primary segmentation and
        describe the alternative. Do not convert a boundary disagreement into a code
        disagreement: the coders may agree about every mathematical idea but disagree
        about whether the response contains one episode or two.
      </p>
      <remark xml:id="rem-edge-nesting-interim">
        <title>Current Interim Rule for Nested Structure</title>
        <p>
          The final retention policy for nested episodes remains open. Until a versioned
          protocol replaces this interim rule, record every identifiable episode, mark
          relations with the current sheet values (<c>chained(k)</c>,
          <c>independent</c>, or blank for a lone episode), note detected nesting, and
          do not silently collapse or drop a level. Put a proposed alternative in the
          notes. This preserves the structure needed to apply a later policy uniformly.
        </p>
      </remark>
    </paragraphs>
    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-decision-interpretation">
      <title>Component Interpretation</title>
      <p>
Begin with a literal inventory of the words, symbols, and calculations in the
response. Then identify the criterion, link, and judgment supported by that
evidence. Apply the canonical completeness and link-status definitions from
<xref ref="ch-episode-model" text="title"/>. In particular, an absent link
does not make an episode incomplete when both criterion and judgment are
identifiable.
</p>
<p>
A computed result may be only an operation in one response and the criterion
of an episode in another. Ask whether the student uses the result to evaluate,
classify, or decide something. Preserve genuinely ambiguous roles rather than
resolving them by preference.
</p>
</paragraphs>
    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-decision-matching">
      <title>Matching Decisions</title>
      <p>
        Match an episode against the full codebook entry: definition, inclusion and
        exclusion rules, examples, near misses, confusable codes, and the
        question-specific criterion-to-judgment relationship. A shared keyword is not
        enough. A response mentioning pivots may rely on a pivot count, a comparison
        between pivots and variables, consistency, dimension, or some combination of
        these ideas.
      </p>
      <p>
        Distinguish two failure types:
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title>Out of vocabulary</title>
            <p>
              The response uses relevant language or a mathematical proposition that
              the controlled vocabulary does not represent adequately.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>No match</title>
            <p>
              The episode can be represented using existing terms, but no current
              codebook entry adequately classifies its control or criterion-to-judgment
              relationship.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>
      <p>
        Neither case should be forced into the nearest code. Preserve the student's
        wording, identify whether the problem concerns a synonym, a new proposition, a
        new control structure, an overly narrow code, or reasoning outside the project's
        scope, and route it for review. A single unusual response may motivate a
        proposal, but new terms and codes should be accepted only after review across
        similar and near-miss cases.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-decision-correctness">
      <title>Correctness and Goal-Coverage Decisions</title>
      <p>
Apply the canonical correctness and goal-coverage rules from
<xref ref="ch-episode-model" text="title"/> and
<xref ref="ch-coding-workflow" text="title"/>. Evaluate criterion truth,
inferential validity, and question-goal coverage separately. A correct final
answer does not validate an unsupported link, and a sound local episode does
not establish complete goal coverage.
</p>
</paragraphs>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-edge-case-playbook">
    <title>Edge-Case Playbook</title>
    <p>
      The table below gives the default action for frequent response types. It does not
      replace the current coding protocol. The confidence column gives a normal
      expectation, not a mandatory score: clear evidence can make an unusual response
      high confidence, while missing question context can make an ordinary response low
      confidence.
    </p>
    <table xml:id="tab-edge-case-playbook">
      <title>Common edge cases and the evidence-preserving response.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="18%"/>
        <col width="52%"/>
        <col width="30%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell><p>You see</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Required action</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Escalate when</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Blank or whitespace only</p></cell>
          <cell>
            <p>
              Preserve one response record with no episode content; set <c>complete</c>=0 and note <q>blank.</q>
            </p>
            <p><em>Typical confidence:</em> <c>3</c></p>
          </cell>
          <cell><p>The record appears truncated or data loss is possible.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Bare answer with no justification</p></cell>
          <cell>
            <p>Record the stated judgment, leave the criterion missing, set <c>complete</c>=0, and note <q>bare answer.</q></p>
            <p><em>Typical confidence:</em> <c>3</c></p>
          </cell>
          <cell><p>The answer may contain compressed mathematical evidence rather than a bare judgment.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Restatement or <q>I don't know</q></p></cell>
          <cell>
            <p>Preserve the response record, set <c>complete</c>=0, and state what occurred.</p>
            <p><em>Typical confidence:</em> <c>3</c></p>
          </cell>
          <cell><p>The restatement appears to add a criterion or interpretation.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Pure computation with no verdict</p></cell>
          <cell>
            <p>Treat it as operations only. Do not create an episode unless the response uses a result to support a judgment.</p>
            <p><em>Typical confidence:</em> <c>2-3</c></p>
          </cell>
          <cell><p>It is unclear whether a displayed result functions as an unstated judgment.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Hedged or conditional reasoning</p></cell>
          <cell>
            <p>Identify whether the hedge itself states a criterion and judgment. Code it when both are supported; otherwise preserve it as incomplete.</p>
            <p><em>Typical confidence:</em> <c>1-2</c></p>
          </cell>
          <cell><p>Several mathematical readings of the condition are plausible.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Unfamiliar or off-standard terminology</p></cell>
          <cell>
            <p>Code the episode structure normally, copy the term verbatim into <c>oov_note</c>, and do not translate it silently.</p>
            <p><em>Typical confidence:</em> <c>1-2</c></p>
          </cell>
          <cell><p>The term may represent a new control, a synonym, or reasoning outside scope.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Theorem or proposition cited only by number</p></cell>
          <cell>
            <p>Record the citation verbatim as the cited basis and flag the reference. Do not import the theorem's content unless the study instructions authorize it.</p>
            <p><em>Typical confidence:</em> <c>2</c></p>
          </cell>
          <cell><p>The cited result is needed to determine the link or lies outside the approved reference materials.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Correct answer supported by an invalid link</p></cell>
          <cell>
            <p>Code the episode normally and assign criterion and link correctness separately. Resist the halo effect of the final answer.</p>
            <p><em>Typical confidence:</em> <c>2-3</c></p>
          </cell>
          <cell><p>Validity depends on an unstated condition or disputed normative reference.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Two defensible readings</p></cell>
          <cell>
            <p>Record the primary reading, describe the alternative, set confidence no higher than 2, and preserve the competing evidence.</p>
            <p><em>Typical confidence:</em> <c>1-2</c></p>
          </cell>
          <cell><p>The readings would change segmentation, code assignment, or correctness.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Several plausible codes for one episode</p></cell>
          <cell>
            <p>Choose a primary code only when one definition is better supported; record the remaining plausible codes in <c>alt_codes</c>.</p>
            <p><em>Typical confidence:</em> <c>1-2</c></p>
          </cell>
          <cell><p>The definitions overlap or no principled preference is available.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell><p>Existing vocabulary but no code</p></cell>
          <cell>
            <p>Record <term>no match</term>; do not manufacture an OOV label or choose the nearest code.</p>
            <p><em>Typical confidence:</em> <c>2</c></p>
          </cell>
          <cell><p>The pattern recurs or suggests that an existing code is too narrow.</p></cell>
        </row>
        </tabular>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-disagreement">
    <title>Disagreement and Adjudication</title>
    <p>
      Disagreement is informative only when the project records what the coders
      disagreed about. Begin with the earliest workflow decision at which the two coding
      records diverge. A later difference may disappear once that earlier difference is
      resolved. For example, different code assignments may be consequences of
      different episode boundaries rather than independent matching disagreements.
    </p>
    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-disagreement-types">
      <title>Classifying the Earliest Disagreement</title>
      <table xml:id="tab-disagreement-taxonomy">
        <title>Disagreement types used during comparison and calibration.</title>
        <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
          <col width="22%"/>
          <col width="78%"/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell><p>Type</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Meaning</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>SCREEN</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Different screening decision.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>BOUNDARY</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Different number or boundary of episodes.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>CRITERION</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Different interpretation of <m>C_k</m>.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>LINK</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Different interpretation or evidence status for <m>I_k</m>.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>JUDGMENT</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Different interpretation of <m>J_k</m>.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>RELATION</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Different chained, independent, nested, or unresolved relation.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>MATCH</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Different primary or alternate code assignment.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>CORRECTNESS</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Different criterion-truth or link-validity annotation.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>COVERAGE</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Different assessment of the goals addressed.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>VOCABULARY</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Different OOV, canonical-term, or no-match treatment.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>POLICY</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Different coding conventions or protocol versions applied.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><p><c>OTHER</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>A disagreement not captured above; explain it in the note.</p></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
      <p>
        More than one label may apply, but identify the earliest one. Do not begin by
        asking which coder is correct. First determine whether the coders noticed
        different evidence, interpreted the same evidence differently, used different
        mathematical references, or applied different rules.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-disagreement-notes">
      <title>Writing an Evidence-Based Note</title>
      <p>
        A useful note is brief enough to scan but specific enough that another reviewer
        can reconstruct the issue. Include:
        <ol>
          <li>the decision being recorded;</li>
          <li>the exact response evidence supporting it;</li>
          <li>the competing reading, when one exists;</li>
          <li>the question context or rule consulted; and</li>
          <li>the resulting action or unresolved status.</li>
        </ol>
      </p>
      <paragraphs xml:id="par-edge-note-template">
        <title>Compact Note Template</title>
        <program language="text">
          Decision: [screening, boundary, component, match, correctness, or coverage]
          Evidence: [exact words, symbols, or calculation]
          Alternative: [competing reading, or none]
          Reference: [question fact, codebook entry, or protocol rule]
          Action: [coded result, escalation, or unresolved]
        </program>
      </paragraphs>
      <p>
        Prefer notes such as <q>The judgment is missing; the response states only that a
        free variable exists</q> or <q>Two episodes are present because the first judgment
        becomes the criterion of the second.</q> Avoid unsupported claims such as
        <q>The student understands,</q> <q>This is obviously what the student meant,</q>
        or <q>The response seems wrong.</q>
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-disagreement-adjudication">
      <title>Adjudication Procedure</title>
      <p>
        Adjudication is a structured comparison, not a negotiation toward the quickest
        compromise. For each disagreement:
        <ol>
          <li>return to the original question and response;</li>
          <li>identify the earliest disagreement type;</li>
          <li>state the evidence for each interpretation;</li>
          <li>consult the governing codebook, normative reference, and protocol version;</li>
          <li>adopt a decision only when one interpretation is better supported; and</li>
          <li>record whether the case indicates a needed revision to the coding materials.</li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <p>
        Preserve the original proposal, the human decision, and the 
        rationale according to the assigned review procedure. Record 
        an evidence-based rationale for every override so later 
        analysis can distinguish tool error, coder disagreement,
        ambiguity, codebook weakness, and missing vocabulary.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-disagreement-unresolved">
      <title>Preserving an Unresolved Case</title>

      <p>
        Leave a case unresolved when the available evidence and governing materials do
        not support one interpretation over the alternatives. Unresolved is a valid
        analytic status, not a failure to complete the work. Record the competing
        interpretations, the evidence for each, the missing information or policy, and
        the decision that cannot yet be made.
      </p>

      <p>
        Do not create a new rule for one response during an active coding round. A
        recurring pattern may justify a proposed vocabulary term, codebook revision,
        exclusion rule, worked example, or protocol clarification. Test the proposed
        revision on the original case, similar cases, and at least one near miss. Adopt
        it only through the project's versioned revision process; earlier records remain
        tied to the rules under which they were coded.
      </p>

      <p>
        A well-documented unresolved case can later support calibration, reliability
        analysis, codebook refinement, and evaluation of the computational pipeline.
        Forced agreement removes precisely the evidence needed to understand where the
        method is fragile.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-edge-final-checklist">
    <title>Edge-Case Review Checklist</title>
    <p>
      Before closing or escalating a difficult case, ask:
      <ol>
        <li><p>What exact decision is difficult?</p></li>
        <li><p>What visible evidence supports each reading?</p></li>
        <li><p>What current rule or resource was consulted?</p></li>
        <li><p>Should the case be resolved, escalated, or preserved as unresolved?</p></li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </section>

  <conclusion>
    <p>
      Edge cases are where the analytic framework, codebook, protocol, and
      computational tool are tested most directly. Careful handling requires more than
      selecting a label: the coder must locate the difficult decision, preserve the
      student's evidence, separate competing interpretations, and record why the case
      was resolved or left open. These records make disagreement analyzable and allow
      the project to improve its methods without erasing the conditions under which
      earlier decisions were made.
    </p>
  </conclusion>
</chapter>